      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to determine the largest 4-digit number and then adjust it to meet the given congruence condition.\n\nFirst, identify the largest 4-digit number. The largest 4-digit number is 9999. We need to find the largest integer \\(x\\) such that \\(x \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\) and \\(x \\leq 9999\\).\n\nWe can express \\(x\\) in the form:\n\\[ x = 22k + 15 \\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. We need to find the largest \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\nCalculate the division:\n\\[ k \\leq 453.8181\\ldots \\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the greatest integer less than or equal to 453.8181, which is 453. Therefore, we set:\n\\[ k = 453 \\]\nNow, substitute \\(k = 453\\) back into the expression for \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = 22 \\cdot 453 + 15 \\]\nCalculate the product:\n\\[ 22 \\cdot 453 = 9966 \\]\nAdd 15:\n\\[ x = 9966 + 15 = 9981 \\]\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9981} \\]",
