      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we need to determine the largest 4-digit integer \\(N\\) such that \\(N \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\). This means we can write \\(N\\) in the form:\n\n\\[ N = 22k + 15 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\(k\\). We need \\(N\\) to be a 4-digit number, so:\n\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, solve the inequality for the lower bound:\n\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\]\n\\[ 985 \\leq 22k \\]\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\approx 44.77 \\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.77, which is 45. Therefore, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 45. Now substitute \\(k = 45\\) into the expression for \\(N\\):\n\n\\[ N = 22 \\cdot 45 + 15 = 990 + 15 = 1005 \\]\n\nNext, we need to check if \\(N = 1005\\) is indeed a 4-digit number and satisfies the congruence condition. Calculate \\(1005 \\mod 22\\):\n\n\\[ 1005 \\div 22 \\approx 45.227 \\]\n\\[ 1005 = 22 \\cdot 45 + 5 \\]\n\\[ 1005 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{22} \\]\n\nSince \\(1005 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{22}\\) and \\(5 \\equiv -17 \\pmod{22}\\) (because \\(-17 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{22}\\)), and \\(15 \\equiv -7 \\pmod{22}\\) (because \\(-7 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\)), it turns out that \\(1005 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\\).\n\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1005}\n\\]",
